btieiso tie_iso 0 V = (v(tie_sel_in_n)-v(tie_samp_tp_n))/vdd_val
* tie: input pinned exactly on the free-MSB threshold for the whole
* run. No code check -- either adjacent code is a correct answer to
* an exact tie. The measured claim is the cadence.
vtiein tie_vinp 0 dc {vcm}
vtiecm tie_vinn 0 dc {vcm}
* Gated by the SETTLED drdy, like b<tag>err above: c9..c0 hold their
* POWER-UP value (all zero, i.e. code 0) until the first ph14 load,
* and an earlier draft of this deck measured tie_dev
* unconditionally -- which made every run report a spurious code-0
* deviation of 512 for the ~875 ns before the first conversion's
* drdy, independent of whether the design was actually correct.
*
* CORRECTED BY DR-0031 (issue #327). The earlier form of this
* comment claimed that gating on drdy alone 'restricts the
* measurement to windows where the register holds a completed
* conversion's result'. It does not, and issue #337 measured it not
* doing so: `assign drdy = ph[15]` and c9..c0 load on the SAME clock
* edge, so the raw gate opens as the data starts moving. At
* ff_27c_3.63v the deck's 512 is at conversion #1 -- the same
* pre-first-drdy code-0 artifact that sentence claimed had been
* removed, narrowed from ~875 ns to ~4 ps rather than removed. The
* settling guard is what actually makes the sentence true.
btiedev tie_dev 0 V = min(v(tie_drdy),v(tie_drdyg))>vth ? abs(v(tie_code)-512) : 0

* Externally-supplied net(s) this subckt's cells bind but nothing
* inside it drives. Without .global, SPICE scopes these LOCALLY to
* each instance -- the DUT would sit unpowered at ~0 V and every
* symptom would read as a solver/power-up problem (issue #282).
.global vdd_gate

